-# GEO Workbench architecture — foundation v0.1
-
-This is the first working slice, not a production enterprise platform. It establishes evidence contracts, dependency boundaries, persistent client records, cue scoring, prompt generation and manual observations. Automated crawl, provider sampling, organization roles and CMS publishing are later slices.
-
-## Dependency rule
-
-UI → HTTP adapter → application services → domain contracts and rules.
-Infrastructure adapters implement domain ports. The domain imports no React, Cloudflare, ORM, provider SDK or CMS package. The composition root is `src/adapters/http.ts`. The current runtime is React/Vinext + a Cloudflare Worker + D1; domain and application modules can move to Node without changing their behavior.
-
-| Module | Responsibility | Replaceable implementation |
+| Layer | Current responsibility | Replacement path |
 | --- | --- | --- |
-| `src/core/contracts.ts` | Canonical Client, Audit, Observation; repository, crawler, provider, CMS and queue ports | TypeScript contracts today; language-neutral HTTP/event schemas for future cross-language services |
-| `src/core/scoring.ts` | Versioned cue rubric, prompt templates, observed mention/citation calculations | Rules can evolve under new versions without modifying historical results |
-| `src/application/workbench.ts` | Owner access checks, audit orchestration, observation import | Runtime-independent application service |
-| `src/adapters/d1-repository.ts` | Prepared SQL, persistent storage and bounded reads | PostgreSQL, another database or a service implementing Repository |
-| `src/adapters/http.ts` | Validation, bounded request bodies, identity, origin checks, composition | HTTP framework and hosting identity adapter |
-| `app/workbench-ui.tsx` | Client workspace | Any UI consuming the versioned API |
-
-The existing Crawler, AIProvider, CMSConnector and JobQueue interfaces are extension seams, not connected services. Any CMS can integrate if an adapter maps its content, identity, versions and permissions into these contracts. Changing language or hosting still requires adapter work; plug compatibility is not automatic.
-
-## Storage and security boundary
-
-New deployment is owner-private. Platform sign-in supplies a trusted user identifier; the host must strip spoofed identity headers before forwarding requests. The worker is not intended to be exposed directly on an untrusted ingress. Each client and record query includes `owner`; application services verify client ownership before reading or writing child records. Writes require a same-origin Origin header. SQL values are bound, request bodies are limited to 600 KB, HTML is limited to 250,000 characters, and manual imports to 20 observations per batch.
+| `app`, `frontend` | Workspace, client selection, query collector, evidence view | Another UI consuming versioned HTTP APIs |
+| `src/server/node-server.ts` | Composition, authentication, origin checks, request validation, adapter registration | Another HTTP framework or hosting runtime |
+| `src/application/workbench.ts` | Client access, audits, manual observation import | Reuse against another repository |
+| `src/application/observation-runner.ts` | Owner check, request replay, provider orchestration, immutable observation save | Invoke through a future durable worker |
+| `src/core` | Client/evidence contracts, query generation, scoring, visibility | Stable domain with no React, database SDK, provider SDK or CMS dependency |
+| `src/server/sqlite-repository.ts` | Repository and exact observation lookup ports | PostgreSQL adapter implementing both ports |
+| `src/server/providers/openai-provider.ts` | Credentialed HTTP transport and Responses API parsing | More adapters implementing `AIProvider` |
+| `src/adapters/d1-repository.ts`, `app/api` | Original hosted adapter reference; unused by standalone build | Wire a separate composition root deliberately |
 
-This is **user isolation**, not full enterprise multitenancy. For organization teams, add workspace, membership, role and resource policy tables; resolve tenant membership on every request and worker job. Never accept a browser-supplied tenant as authorization. Add database constraints or PostgreSQL row-level policies as defense in depth, and transactional outbox records for queued work.
+Provider configuration lives only on the server. `/api/v1/providers` returns id, label, model and whether credentials were supplied. The application runner does not know API keys, HTTP vendor details or model-specific response schemas. Add a provider by implementing the port and registering it; scoring and client storage stay unchanged.
 
-Clients and audit/observation envelopes are stored in indexed tables. JSON envelopes simplify the first slice; replace them with indexed normalized page/run/query/observation tables when reporting requires joins. Current list limits: 100 clients, latest 200 audits and latest 200 observations per client. Pagination, deduplication, retention and deletion are not yet implemented.
+## Collection and persistence
 
-Imported HTML is analyzed as inert text and discarded after analysis. Immutable audit records retain dimensions, evidence explanations, URL, source, timestamp, SHA-256 input hash and rubric version. The hash proves input equality, not authenticity or recoverability. Raw evidence retention should later use object storage with tenant keys, encryption, retention and provenance. Do not send imported client content to a model until the provider and data handling are configured.
+A run accepts one prompt, provider ID and UUID request ID. The application checks client ownership before contacting the provider. A deterministic SHA-256 record ID scopes the UUID to the owner/client. Completed replays return existing evidence; active duplicate calls are rejected. Two requests may run concurrently per process. A successful provider response is stored atomically; failures do not create observations. The same SQLite schema remains compatible with v0.1.1 data. New optional provider metadata is stored in the existing JSON envelope.
 
-## Scoring and measurement
+The OpenAI adapter requests web search, caps generated output at 2,048 tokens and applies a 60-second deadline. It checks completed response/search status, preserves the model, original question, answer, citations, inline citation ranges, provider response ID and observation time. It does not inject a target brand into the query. Server credentials are never returned to the UI. No automatic retries, scheduler, currency budget or cross-process lease is implemented.
 
-The nine rubric weights total 100. HTML imports assess five dimensions totaling 70: page structure (10), brand clarity (15), answer structure (20), reference cues (15), structured data cues (10). Crawler access (15), freshness (5), external authority (5) and monitoring coverage (5) remain unknown.
+SQLite uses WAL, foreign keys, parameterized queries and transactions. Each read filters owner; application services verify client access before writes. List limits remain 100 clients and 200 latest records of each kind. JSON imports retain their manual source. Actual observations and imports share the same visibility calculation. Imported HTML is analyzed as inert text and discarded; the audit retains evidence findings, hash and rubric version.
 
-`assessed score = round(sum(weight × cue score) / sum(assessed weights))`
+## Authentication and deployments
 
-Unknowns are excluded and coverage is shown beside the score. This is a proprietary heuristic for implementation review, not a validated predictor of search ranking. A single external link satisfies a reference *cue*; it does not establish source quality. JSON-LD checks syntax and presence of a typed node; it does not validate schema semantics. The 150-word signal is a provisional rubric cue, not a search-engine requirement or recommendation to add filler. Regex-based extraction handles conventional quoted attributes, not arbitrary malformed HTML, DOM execution, CSS visibility or entity normalization. Improve parser coverage and calibrate the rubric against reviewed examples before commercial use.
+Local mode binds to loopback and enforces a localhost Host header. Cloud mode requires Basic credentials and HTTPS at the reverse proxy. Writes require an allowed Origin, input schemas reject extra fields, and bodies are bounded. Identity is derived from configured authentication, not spoofable request headers. When moving a database between runtimes, preserve the owner username to retain access.
 
-Google's official guidance says foundational SEO and useful original content matter, with no special structured data requirement for generative search. Do not market structured data as a guaranteed GEO boost. OpenAI distinguishes search discovery controls from training crawler controls; the next crawler must assess them separately.
+The portable server is the active composition root. The hosted Site is not changed by this release. Reference adapters are not automatically compatible with every new application port; for example, hosted collection needs its own observation lookup and secret registration before the new runner can be wired there.
 
-Mention rate is the percentage of imported response strings with a case-insensitive brand match using Unicode letter/number boundaries. Citation rate is the percentage with at least one supplied citation URL on the client domain/subdomain. Both denominators use up to the 200 latest imported rows, across all engines/surfaces. The app retains model, surface, prompt and observed timestamp. Rates describe that sample; they are not population estimates, consumer UI rankings, causal impact or recommendations. Manual citations and responses are user-supplied and not independently verified. Duplicate imports count twice. No GSOV, sentiment, inferred recommendation, projections or competitive rank is fabricated.
+## Evidence interpretation
 
-## Scaling sequence and acceptance gates
+Readiness uses five assessed HTML cue dimensions totaling 70% of the rubric; unknown dimensions stay null and are excluded from the normalized weighted score. Scores assess implementation cues, not ranking or content quality. Brand matching uses the canonical brand name and explicitly configured aliases, with case-insensitive Unicode boundaries and flexible whitespace between words. It does not guess aliases. Citation matching requires the client hostname or a true subdomain. API results and consumer search experiences are distinct samples. Current rates combine the latest 200 observations and are descriptive, not population estimates or causal impact.
 
